{{also|pus}}
==English==

===Pronunciation===
* {{enPR|po͝os}}, {{IPA|/pʊs/|lang=en}}
* {{rhymes|ʊs|lang=en}}

===Etymology 1===
From a Common {{etyl|gem|en}} word for cat, perhaps ultimately imitative of a sound made to get its attention. Akin to {{etyl|nl|-}} {{m|nl|poes||puss, cat," slang for "vagina}}, West Frisian {{m|fy|poes}}, {{etyl|nds|-}} {{m|nds|Puus}}, {{m|nds|Puuskatte}}, Danish {{m|da|pus}}, dialectal {{etyl|sv|-}} {{m|sv|kattepus}}, {{etyl|no|-}} {{m|no|pus}}.

Found also in several other European, North Africa and West Asian languages; compare {{etyl|ro|-}} {{m|ro|pisică}}.

====Noun====
{{en-noun|es}}

# {{label|en|informal}} A [[cat]].
#: ''Our local theatre is showing '''Puss''' in Boots.''
# A girl or young woman.
# {{label|en|dated|hunting}} A [[hare]].<!--Webster 1913-->
# {{label|en|vulgar|slang}} [[vulva|Vulva]] (female genitalia).
# {{label|en|vulgar|slang|chiefly|_|North America}} A [[coward]], a [[wuss]]; someone unable to stand up for himself.

=====Synonyms=====
* {{sense|cat}} {{l|en|moggie}}/[[moggy]]

=====Related terms=====
* {{l|en|pussy}}

====Derived terms====
* {{l|en|puss out}}

===Etymology 2===
Of {{etyl|cel|en}} origin, from or akin to {{etyl|ga|en}} {{m|ga|pus||mouth, lip}}, from {{etyl|mga|en}} {{m|mga|bus|}}.

====Noun====
{{en-noun|es}}

# {{label|en|slang}} The [[mouth]].
#: ''She gave him a slap in the '''puss'''.''

=====Synonyms=====
* {{sense|mouth}} {{l|en|cakehole}}, {{l|en|gob}}, {{l|en|mush}}, {{l|en|trap}}

===Anagrams===
* {{l|en|PSUs}}, {{l|en|sups}}, {{l|en|USPs}}, {{l|en|USPS}}

